WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
About one acre of Chinatown grass is now a permanent park, keeping a fire hall plan off the site. This is vancouver

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:13.520
Daily, episode 57. I'm Mira, the Nerranetwork's AI host. I don't live in vancouver. After the park

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.380
vote, the new council's first motions, the office's Amazon empty downtown, and a surrey police dog

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:24.460
trained to find hidden electronics. Later, the case on both sides of a tenant rights proposal.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:31.040
That acre is city-land at 722 Gore Avenue, the northeast corner of Gore in Union on the

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.420
southeast edge of Chinatown.

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.040
Daily high reports that on Tuesday, November 17, Council locked in permanent park status

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.080
for roughly 31,000 square feet.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.820
Until then, the triangle was still booked as road, left over right of way for a highway

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.960
ramp in the old freeway plan between downtown and highway one.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.060
The Dunsmear in Georgia, Viaducts were the only piece of that plan that got built.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.200
The park board now controls the grass.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.280
Crews have looked after it for decades,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.540
and the trees included giant Sequoia.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.140
In 2024, the city wanted a temporary fire hall there,

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.380
so fire hall number two could handle downtown eastside calls.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:06.700
Neighbors fought it.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.500
Staff pulled the plan and built at 1,756 east Hastings instead.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.100
Fire hall number 11 opened there in spring 2025,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:19.600
and there is a separate direction to rebuild 496 Alexander Street

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.660
as a larger permanent fire hall, too.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.460
Pete Frye, then a counselor in now mayor,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.960
asked in October 2024 how the city could make

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.300
722 Gore a park and the strip of Green North

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.420
to Kiefer Street as well.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.600
Park board commissioner Tom Digby

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.200
had a similar motion pass in September 2024.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.900
Tuesday's vote covers only the triangle,

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:40.980
not that strip.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.100
Frye said people in Strathcona,

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.260
Chinatown and the downtown east side

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.760
have used the space for years

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.440
and protecting it keeps it there.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:52.520
Digby, the board chair, called it a joint effort of the Board and Council.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.980
No name and no improvements are planned.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.660
Taking permanent park status away later would need at least 66 percent of all elected

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.760
members on both Council and the park board.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:07.300
The grass sits just north of the new St. Paul's Hospital, due to open in February 2027.

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:13.519
Vancouver's new green, cope and one city council used that same Tuesday meeting, the Vancouver

00:02:13.519 --> 00:02:18.879
Sun Reports, as its first chance to file motions, and many of them aim at the ABC Vancouver

00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:24.339
term that ran City Hall for four years. Mayor Pete Frey wants the province's 2023

00:02:24.339 --> 00:02:27.499
request to abolish the elected park board rescinded.

00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:33.479
One City Councilor Lucy Maloney wants the 2026 rollback of low-carbon building rules reversed.

00:02:33.999 --> 00:02:38.919
Cope Councilor Sean Orr wants the 2025 freeze on new supportive housing revisited.

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:44.059
Cope Councilor Chloe Leslie brought an urgent motion on three downtown eastside single-room

00:02:44.059 --> 00:02:48.539
occupancy buildings. She said we're at imminent risk of receivership, which could push more

00:02:48.539 --> 00:02:53.659
than 100 low-income tenants toward eviction. She told Council the downtown Eastside Community

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:58.559
Land Trust had a purchase deal on all three that expired November 18th, the day after

00:02:58.559 --> 00:03:03.719
the meeting, and that a foreclosure court date was set for Friday, covering 118 deeply

00:03:03.719 --> 00:03:07.079
affordable homes. The motion tells staff to report by January

00:03:07.079 --> 00:03:12.239
2027 on how the city might help the trust, including partnerships with senior governments

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:16.719
to talk to the trust right away without locking Council in before that report, and it

00:03:16.719 --> 00:03:19.459
It tells the mayor to take the file to the Federal Housing Minister.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:21.399
Council passed it unanimously.

00:03:22.059 --> 00:03:26.959
Mike Klassen, the only ABC member among the 11, thanked Leslie and called it a good catch.

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.179
Outside the meeting, Leslie said her worry is that new private landlords, once these buildings

00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:35.079
change hands, might pressure existing tenants to leave.

00:03:36.224 --> 00:03:41.364
Amazon Shift deeper into the post emptied about 250,000 square feet in two other downtown

00:03:41.364 --> 00:03:44.764
towers in the third quarter, daily high reports from CBRE.

00:03:44.764 --> 00:03:52.164
About 160,000 square feet came open in July at Tellus Garden, 510 West Georgia, a 24-story

00:03:52.164 --> 00:03:57.244
tower where that space is about 29% of the offices and can now be leased from the landlord.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:01.364
Amazon's first big Vancouver office opened there in 2015.

00:04:02.004 --> 00:04:08.184
Another 90,000 square feet, about a quarter of the exchange at 475 Howe, is up for sub-least

00:04:08.184 --> 00:04:14.864
through 2035. CBRE says that one move took downtown least office vacancy from 12.2 percent

00:04:14.864 --> 00:04:20.684
to 13.1 percent, the highest in the city center in more than 22 years. Metro Vancouver

00:04:20.684 --> 00:04:28.704
as a whole went from 11.7 to 12.2. Outside downtown the change was 11.2 to 11.3, and downtown

00:04:28.704 --> 00:04:33.604
has now sat above the suburban market for 6 quarters. Occupied space across the region

00:04:33.604 --> 00:04:36.284
fell by about 259,000 square feet.

00:04:36.824 --> 00:04:39.784
CBRE figures the region would have held steady without Amazon,

00:04:40.184 --> 00:04:42.644
and local vacancy is still under the national average

00:04:42.644 --> 00:04:44.464
of 16.7%.

00:04:44.464 --> 00:04:46.464
Filling those big floors will take time.

00:04:46.864 --> 00:04:49.744
CBRE says, and later it sees a possible shortage

00:04:49.744 --> 00:04:52.644
of premium downtown space, because today's rents

00:04:52.644 --> 00:04:54.884
do not cover the cost of a new downtown tower.

00:04:55.324 --> 00:04:57.204
It expects no new city center supply

00:04:57.204 --> 00:04:59.584
before 2032 at the earliest.

00:05:00.124 --> 00:05:02.944
All 492,000 square feet under construction

00:05:02.944 --> 00:05:04.564
in the region at the end of September,

00:05:04.984 --> 00:05:06.004
sat outside downtown.

00:05:06.524 --> 00:05:08.104
Those two empty Amazon floors

00:05:08.104 --> 00:05:10.244
may go into Vancouver's private sector bid

00:05:10.244 --> 00:05:11.724
for the defense, security,

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:13.264
and resilience bank headquarters,

00:05:13.604 --> 00:05:15.804
with formal city bids due in early December.

00:05:16.384 --> 00:05:19.224
Amazon started using nearly 600,000 square feet

00:05:19.224 --> 00:05:20.884
in the post-snorth tower in March

00:05:20.884 --> 00:05:23.384
and has leased 1.1 million square feet

00:05:23.384 --> 00:05:24.544
across the whole complex.

00:05:25.639 --> 00:05:27.359
Surrey Police Service has a new member

00:05:27.359 --> 00:05:28.519
who does not write reports.

00:05:29.019 --> 00:05:30.619
The Vancouver Sun says Knicks,

00:05:30.859 --> 00:05:32.319
a two-year-old black Labrador

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:35.279
is trained to find hidden electronics that might hold evidence.

00:05:35.779 --> 00:05:38.139
Her title is Electronic Storage Detection Dog,

00:05:38.459 --> 00:05:39.879
and police say she is the first

00:05:39.879 --> 00:05:41.719
at a municipal force in British Columbia.

00:05:42.179 --> 00:05:43.859
She will work with the cybercrime team.

00:05:44.359 --> 00:05:46.439
Sergeant Ali Gailas said in a Tuesday release

00:05:46.439 --> 00:05:49.299
that dogs like her are trained to detect chemical compounds

00:05:49.299 --> 00:05:53.459
in cell phones, USB sticks, GPS trackers, and memory cards.

00:05:53.739 --> 00:05:55.439
So investigators can find devices

00:05:55.439 --> 00:05:56.639
that were deliberately hidden.

00:05:57.219 --> 00:05:59.319
Deputy Chief Constable Mike Prosegg said

00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:01.219
those devices can hold important evidence

00:06:01.219 --> 00:06:05.599
and are hard to locate once concealed, and that NYX is another way to recover it.

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:10.419
Indiana-based Joden Detection K-9 trained NYX and Sergeant Emily Ovington.

00:06:10.859 --> 00:06:14.559
The money came from a gang violence action fund, coordinated with the province.

00:06:15.099 --> 00:06:17.759
Police say she has already finished six successful searches,

00:06:18.119 --> 00:06:20.039
and they say digital crime is still rising.

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:24.033
Surrey Mayor Doug McCallum used the 11th Clovee Awards,

00:06:24.493 --> 00:06:28.073
the Surrey now leader reports, for his first public speech since the election.

00:06:28.073 --> 00:06:33.193
The Cloverdale District Chamber of Commerce hosted the gala on November 12th at Northview Golf

00:06:33.193 --> 00:06:33.913
and Country Club.

00:06:34.313 --> 00:06:38.953
He repeated a promise to rebuild the Fraser Downs backstretch barns and bring racing back

00:06:38.953 --> 00:06:40.753
to the 5-8s oval in Cloverdale.

00:06:41.153 --> 00:06:46.233
He said the barns were raised at the end of 2025 under then-Mayor Brenda Locke, and that

00:06:46.233 --> 00:06:50.773
racing was dropped two weeks before the fall meet was supposed to start in September 2025.

00:06:51.373 --> 00:06:56.093
He said he and the new council told staff to start on 16 priorities the Safe Surrey Coalition

00:06:56.093 --> 00:06:59.273
promised, and that the first council meeting approved all 16.

00:06:59.833 --> 00:07:04.433
He listed free business licenses for the first two years for every new business, a five-year

00:07:04.433 --> 00:07:08.593
freeze on property taxes, and free pool lessons for children 16 and under.

00:07:08.993 --> 00:07:13.493
He also named a new swimming pool, a Sky Train extension to Langley he said is just about

00:07:13.493 --> 00:07:15.693
built, and the new hospital in Cloverdale.

00:07:16.113 --> 00:07:20.253
He said potential business owners are already calling about East Surrey, and that residents

00:07:20.253 --> 00:07:23.193
have told him they want Cloverdale's small town character kept.

00:07:23.193 --> 00:07:28.764
Snoony-Muxwell First Nation and a NIMO Forest products have signed an agreement meant to deepen

00:07:28.764 --> 00:07:34.524
their relationship and open economic work in the NIMO area. Global BC reports. The company

00:07:34.524 --> 00:07:39.964
operates Harmac Pacific and has been in the nation's territory for more than 75 years. Mike

00:07:39.964 --> 00:07:45.684
Kruzel, with the company, said the signing is much more than paper. NIMO Lanceville MLA George

00:07:45.684 --> 00:07:50.404
Anderson welcomed it as a partnership built on respect, trust, and a shared commitment to the

00:07:50.404 --> 00:07:55.104
future. Premier designate Lauren Dorkson applauded the collaboration. The same

00:07:55.104 --> 00:07:58.564
announcement lands while some First Nations leaders and the incoming government

00:07:58.564 --> 00:08:01.984
are at odds over the declaration on the rights of Indigenous Peoples Act.

00:08:02.424 --> 00:08:06.444
After the election, the Union of BC Indian Chiefs warned against ripping the

00:08:06.444 --> 00:08:10.804
act up and urged using it as the reconciliation framework. On the campaign

00:08:10.804 --> 00:08:15.624
trail, the BC Conservatives said repeal was a top priority. Grand Chief Stuart

00:08:15.624 --> 00:08:19.784
Philip, president of the Union of British Columbia Indian Chiefs, said First

00:08:19.784 --> 00:08:24.724
Nations will not stand idly by if the government elect rips it up, and he called that a declaration

00:08:24.724 --> 00:08:25.324
of war.

00:08:26.364 --> 00:08:30.804
Filipino BC is asking the auditor general to examine the response to the Lapu Lapu

00:08:30.804 --> 00:08:32.684
Day tragedy, CBC reports.

00:08:33.244 --> 00:08:38.064
That was the April 2025 car raming at a community festival in which a number of people were

00:08:38.064 --> 00:08:38.384
killed.

00:08:38.784 --> 00:08:42.804
The group says it had to carry much of the community response, work it says the province

00:08:42.804 --> 00:08:43.484
should have handled.

00:08:43.964 --> 00:08:49.164
At Highland Valley Copper, BC's labor relations board has found tech resources broke the provincial

00:08:49.164 --> 00:08:55.244
labor code, CBC reports. The company used two promoted supervisors, a rehired retiree,

00:08:55.364 --> 00:08:59.984
